# bat -- generated by theme-set. Do not edit; edit the template instead.
#   template: theme-src/templates/bat-config.in
#   output:   ~/.config/bat/config
#
# Format is command-line options, one per line, exactly as you would type
# them. This is the path `bat --config-file` reports, so it is bat's own
# location and not an invention of this repo.
#
# WHY A NAMED THEME AND NOT GENERATED COLOURS
#
# bat's themes are Sublime .tmTheme files -- a few hundred lines of XML
# defining a colour per syntax scope. Generating one from the palette would be
# the single largest template here and would need per-language scope knowledge
# to be any good. So the palette instead names the closest built-in theme via
# BAT_THEME, and `bat --list-themes` shows the options.
#
# The alternative worth knowing about: the built-in `ansi` and `base16` themes
# render through the terminal's 16 colours, which kitty already themes from
# this palette -- so those follow a palette switch automatically, at the cost
# of much coarser highlighting. Set BAT_THEME=ansi in a palette if you would
# rather have exact-match colours than good ones.

--theme="@BAT_THEME@"

# numbers  line numbers
# changes  a git gutter marking added/modified lines
# header   the filename above the content
#
# Deliberately not "full": that adds `grid`, which draws box-drawing rules
# around everything and makes copying text out of the terminal messy.
--style="numbers,changes,header"

# Page only when the output does not fit. $PAGER is less with -RFX (see
# ~/.zshenv), so short files print inline and stay on screen.
--paging=auto

# Tabs as 4 spaces, so a file indented with tabs does not render at width 8.
--tabs=4

# Keep italics -- JetBrainsMono Nerd Font ships a real italic face, so
# comments render as intended rather than being synthesised or ignored.
--italic-text=always
